---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { url } from '../lib/site';
import { dgmLanguage } from '../lib/dgm-language.mjs';
import payments from '../examples/payments.dgm?raw';
import events from '../examples/events.dgm?raw';
const examples = [{ id: 'payments', name: 'Payments platform', source: payments }, { id: 'events', name: 'Event-driven orders', source: events }];
---
<Base>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow"><i></i> A LITTLE OPINIONATED. A LOT CLEARER.</span><span class="version mono">OPEN SOURCE / v0.1</span></div>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Complex systems.<br /><span>Diagrams made simple.</span></h1>
      <div class="hero-bottom">
        <div class="hero-description"><p>Let your coding agent make the first draft.<br class="desktop-break" /> Refine it in Studio. Keep it all in a plain text file.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('docs/')}>Meet your new diagram workflow <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
        <Install />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="demo" aria-label="Real diagram examples" data-demo>
      <div class="demo-toolbar"><div class="demo-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Example diagram">{examples.map((example, index) => <button id={`tab-${example.id}`} role="tab" aria-selected={index === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} aria-controls={`example-${example.id}`} tabindex={index === 0 ? 0 : -1} data-example={example.id}><span class="file-icon" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span>{example.name}</button>)}</div><span class="demo-live mono"><i></i> SAME SOURCE. SAME RESULT.</span></div>
      {examples.map((example, index) => <div id={`example-${example.id}`} role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby={`tab-${example.id}`} hidden={index !== 0} tabindex="0" class="example-panel">
        <button class="mobile-source-toggle" data-source-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls={`source-${example.id}`}>Show .dgm source <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></button>
        <div class="source-pane" id={`source-${example.id}`}><div class="pane-heading mono"><span>{example.id}.dgm</span><button class="source-copy" data-copy={example.source} aria-label={`Copy ${example.name} source`}>Copy <span aria-hidden="true">⧉</span></button></div><Code code={example.source.trim()} lang={dgmLanguage} theme="github-dark" /><div class="source-bottom mono">PLAIN TEXT IN. <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></div></div>
        <div class="diagram-pane"><div class="pane-heading mono"><span>THE BIG PICTURE</span><span class="diagram-format">SVG / AUTO LAYOUT</span></div><img src={url(`diagrams/${example.id}-preview.svg`)} alt={`A ${example.name.toLowerCase()} diagram showing a customer connected to three AWS services, with numbered steps inside an AWS Region.`} width="1100" height="450" fetchpriority={index === 0 ? 'high' : 'auto'} /><div class="diagram-bottom"><span><i></i> Rendered by the actual engine</span><a href={url(`diagrams/${example.id}.svg`)} download>Download SVG ↗</a></div></div>
      </div>)}
      <div class="demo-footnote"><span>Real source. Rendered by Dumb Diagrams.</span><a href={url('docs/source-format/')}>Learn the .dgm format ↗</a></div>
    </section>

    <div class="principles-strip" aria-label="Product principles"><span>MADE FOR YOUR WORKFLOW</span><p>Agent-ready</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Local by default</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Versioned with Git</p><b aria-hidden="true">+</b><p>Open source</p></div>

    <section class="workflow section" id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">01 / A SHORTER PATH TO CLARITY</span><h2 id="workflow-title">You think in systems.<br /><span class="muted">We’ll handle the boxes.</span></h2><p>The interesting part is how your architecture works. Not the afternoon spent lining up arrows.</p></div>
      <div class="workflow-grid">
        <article><span class="step-number">01<span>↗</span></span><h3>Describe the architecture.</h3><p>Give your coding agent the services, boundaries, and flow. Your project comes with instructions, an icon catalog, and examples to get it started.</p><a href={url('docs/agent-workflow/')}>Work with an agent <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
        <article><span class="step-number">02<span>→</span></span><h3>Make it your own.</h3><p>Open Studio to move a service, adjust a connector, or edit the source. Your manual decisions stay in the file and carry through to the next render.</p><a href={url('docs/studio/')}>Get to know Studio <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
        <article><span class="step-number">03<span>↓</span></span><h3>Keep it close to the code.</h3><p>Commit the diagram with the change it explains. Review it in a diff. Export an SVG for your README or a PDF for the conversation that comes next.</p><a href={url('docs/exports/')}>See the export options <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="manifesto section" aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
      <div class="manifesto-main"><span class="eyebrow">02 / SMALL FORMAT. STRONG OPINIONS.</span><h2 id="manifesto-title">A diagram should explain your system.<br /><em>Not become another one.</em></h2><p>No hosted workspace to maintain. No mystery layout on the next run. Just a readable file, a deterministic engine, and a little room for your judgment.</p><a class="button light" href={url('docs/how-it-works/')}>Look under the hood <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="manifesto-notes"><article><span class="mono">.dgm</span><h3>The file is the source of truth.</h3><p>Structure and saved visual edits travel together. Studio is a view of your document.</p></article><article><span class="mono">1 → 1</span><h3>Predictable by design.</h3><p>The same source, catalog, and saved view produce the same layout, routes, and SVG.</p></article><article><span class="mono">AWS FIRST</span><h3>Focused, not everything.</h3><p>Built for architecture diagrams. AWS has the most developed grammar and examples today.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq section" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / A FEW GOOD QUESTIONS</span><h2 id="faq-title">The practical bits.</h2><p>A small tool, with clear boundaries.</p></div><div class="faq-list">
      <details><summary>Do I need an AI agent to use it?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No. You can write .dgm files yourself or edit them in Studio. The project includes instructions and examples for coding agents because they make a useful first draft—not because they are required.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Where do my diagrams live?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>On your machine, as .dgm files. Start Studio through the CLI to read and save those files directly. The standalone browser editor uses browser storage and explicit downloads instead. <a href={url('docs/studio/')}>Read about the two modes.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use it for clouds other than AWS?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>The catalog includes Azure, Google Cloud, and cloud-agnostic assets too. AWS currently has the strongest grammar, examples, and visual guidance. Start with that expectation; this is not a promise of identical support across providers.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a whiteboard?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Dumb Diagrams understands services, boundaries, and connections. It gives them an opinionated layout that you can refine. It is not a general illustration tool, live infrastructure inventory, or hosted multiplayer workspace.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it free and open source?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. The project is released under AGPL-3.0-only. Provider icons retain their own upstream terms. You can read the <a href={url('LICENSE.txt')}>project license</a> and <a href={url('ICON-NOTICES.txt')}>icon notices</a>.</p></details>
    </div></section>

    <section class="closing"><div><span class="eyebrow">LESS FRICTION. MORE “OH, I GET IT.”</span><h2>Your next architecture,<br /><em>in a clearer picture.</em></h2></div><a href={url('docs/quick-start/')} class="closing-cta">Make your first diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </main>
</Base>
